1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:04,440
When people decide to move to a new
domain, most of them do the same thing.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:08,100
They buy the new one,
switch on "forwarding" at the registrar,

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:11,940
type the old address,
and land on the new site. And honestly?

4
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,230
Fair enough. It looks done.

5
00:00:16,005 --> 00:00:18,705
But here's what a half-done move does,
a few weeks later.

6
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:21,945
Some old links open with a warning:
"Not secure."

7
00:00:22,305 --> 00:00:25,675
Others dump people on the homepage,
not the page they wanted.

8
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:27,585
Some pages are just… gone.

9
00:00:27,915 --> 00:00:30,525
Google can't follow the move,
so your rankings slip.

10
00:00:30,825 --> 00:00:32,445
And the QR code on your menu?

11
00:00:32,655 --> 00:00:35,265
The link in that email from last year?
Dead.

12
00:00:35,957 --> 00:00:38,927
So how do you move your domain the right
way, and keep it secure?

13
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:41,037
It's simpler than you think.

14
00:00:43,627 --> 00:00:45,847
Three ideas, and then we do it.

15
00:00:47,211 --> 00:00:49,971
Idea one: a redirect is a note on the
door.

16
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:52,971
Your old domain is a house you've moved
out of.

17
00:00:53,241 --> 00:00:54,501
People still show up.

18
00:00:54,861 --> 00:00:57,741
The note says: we moved,
here's the new address.

19
00:00:57,981 --> 00:01:01,401
A good note sends them to the right room,
not just the front door.

20
00:01:01,731 --> 00:01:05,971
The internet has a specific note for this:
a 301.

21
00:01:06,021 --> 00:01:08,161
It means "moved permanently."

22
00:01:08,211 --> 00:01:11,281
Browsers follow it instantly,
and Google reads it as:

23
00:01:11,331 --> 00:01:13,641
update your records, this is the new home.

24
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:17,707
Idea two: DNS is the internet's address
book.

25
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:20,867
Type a domain,
and your browser looks it up in that book

26
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:23,437
to find a number: the server to talk to.

27
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:25,507
Those entries are called records.

28
00:01:25,777 --> 00:01:28,897
Moving the right way means changing a
couple of those records,

29
00:01:29,077 --> 00:01:32,227
so the old domain points at something that
hands out the note.

30
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:35,632
Idea three: the padlock is an ID card.

31
00:01:36,082 --> 00:01:40,432
Over HTTPS, a site shows an ID card first.

32
00:01:40,642 --> 00:01:42,112
It's called a certificate.

33
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:46,342
It proves the site is who it says it is,
and it keeps the connection private.

34
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:48,622
No certificate, no padlock.

35
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:51,982
A warning instead of your page. Here's the
catch.

36
00:01:52,192 --> 00:01:56,672
Your old domain needs its own ID card,
even if all it does is point somewhere

37
00:01:56,722 --> 00:02:00,442
else. The browser checks the ID before it
reads the note.

38
00:02:01,093 --> 00:02:04,913
And browsers are getting stricter:
Chrome is rolling out a warning before it

39
00:02:04,963 --> 00:02:07,093
opens public sites that aren't secure.

40
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:11,273
To be fair to registrars:
some forwarding handles the ID card for

41
00:02:11,323 --> 00:02:14,353
you. Some doesn't,
and the certificate is on you.

42
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:16,453
So, some do, some don't.

43
00:02:16,753 --> 00:02:21,223
Here's how to check:
type your old address with https in front.

44
00:02:21,613 --> 00:02:25,303
Padlock? You're fine. Warning? Keep
watching. That's it.

45
00:02:25,513 --> 00:02:28,993
Three ideas: the note,
the address book, the ID card.

46
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:30,713
Now let's use them.

47
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:33,446
Here's how to do it the right way.

48
00:02:33,656 --> 00:02:37,136
Full disclosure: we make RedirHub,
so that's what I'll use.

49
00:02:37,406 --> 00:02:41,976
It handles the note and the ID card for
you, but the steps are the same idea

50
00:02:42,026 --> 00:02:45,566
anywhere. Old domain: mydomain.com.

51
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:48,236
New site: mybrand.com.

52
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,690
Step one: write the note.

53
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:53,450
Links → Create → Domain Redirect.

54
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,240
Redirect from: mydomain.com.

55
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:01,160
Redirect to: https://mybrand.com.

56
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:03,300
Type: 301.

57
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:04,550
And keep the path on.

58
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:08,030
That's the right-room-not-the-front-door
part. Save.

59
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:09,290
One idea down.

60
00:03:09,953 --> 00:03:11,853
Step two: the address book.

61
00:03:11,903 --> 00:03:15,233
RedirHub shows you exactly which records
to change.

62
00:03:15,473 --> 00:03:20,033
For a root domain, that's two:
an A record, the number to point at,

63
00:03:20,093 --> 00:03:23,813
and a TXT record,
which proves the domain is yours.

64
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:29,393
Plus a CNAME for the www version. Quick
tip.

65
00:03:29,693 --> 00:03:32,963
Before you touch anything in your DNS,
screenshot it.

66
00:03:33,083 --> 00:03:35,543
Thirty seconds now, zero regret later.

67
00:03:36,245 --> 00:03:39,105
Now open your DNS,
wherever your domain lives,

68
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:40,535
and add those records.

69
00:03:40,625 --> 00:03:41,915
Copy and paste.

70
00:03:42,185 --> 00:03:44,015
And copy yours, not mine.

71
00:03:44,315 --> 00:03:47,285
Back in RedirHub,
it checks every few seconds.

72
00:03:47,525 --> 00:03:49,055
The address book updates…

73
00:03:49,205 --> 00:03:51,755
and the ID card is issued automatically.

74
00:03:51,995 --> 00:03:54,195
Two green checks: DNS.

75
00:03:54,245 --> 00:03:55,565
HTTPS.

76
00:03:55,625 --> 00:03:56,895
That's the whole setup.

77
00:03:56,945 --> 00:04:01,108
Idea three took care of itself. Let's test
it.

78
00:04:01,168 --> 00:04:04,018
Open a browser, type mydomain.com…

79
00:04:04,378 --> 00:04:06,508
…and it's still the old site. Hold on.

80
00:04:06,688 --> 00:04:08,248
Your redirect might be fine.

81
00:04:08,518 --> 00:04:10,238
It's your test that's wrong.

82
00:04:11,924 --> 00:04:15,915
Browsers keep copies of pages they've
seen, so you may be looking at yesterday's

83
00:04:15,965 --> 00:04:19,514
site. First rule of testing a move:
a private window.

84
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:21,584
No copies, no leftovers.

85
00:04:21,854 --> 00:04:24,924
Same address, private window…
and there it is.

86
00:04:24,974 --> 00:04:33,062
mybrand.com. Padlock. Three quick checks.

87
00:04:34,372 --> 00:04:37,522
One: the old domain,
with https in front,

88
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:39,412
lands on the new site with a padlock.

89
00:04:39,562 --> 00:04:43,582
Two: an old deep link lands on the same
page, not the homepage.

90
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:48,302
Three: the www version works too. All
three green.

91
00:04:49,597 --> 00:04:51,097
Two things before you go.

92
00:04:51,577 --> 00:04:55,837
A padlock means the connection is private,
not that the site is honest.

93
00:04:56,137 --> 00:04:58,987
It's an ID card,
not a character reference.

94
00:04:59,257 --> 00:05:01,327
And don't let the old domain expire.

95
00:05:01,717 --> 00:05:04,217
If it lapses,
someone else can register it,

96
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:07,747
and every old link and printed QR code
points at them.

97
00:05:08,077 --> 00:05:11,587
Keep renewing it as long as anyone might
still have the old address.

98
00:05:12,284 --> 00:05:17,744
Remember the start? Forwarding on,
old link opens, looks done. Now it is.

